AegisDrone — AI-Based Drone Threat Detection System
A Briefing for Government of India Decision Makers

What Problem Are We Solving?
Imagine you are the security chief at Parliament House, or at a border outpost in Ladakh, or at a nuclear facility in Tarapur.
One day, a small drone appears in the sky.
Is it a journalist's camera drone? A farmer's crop-monitoring drone? Or is it carrying explosives, a biological agent, or a surveillance payload for an enemy?
You have seconds to decide. If you're wrong and shoot down a friendly drone — international incident. If you're wrong and ignore a threat — catastrophe.
Today, India has no automated system that can make this call in real time. Human operators watch radar screens and guess. This project changes that.

What Does AegisDrone Actually Do?
Think of it like a doctor who listens to your heartbeat through a stethoscope.
Every drone — whether friendly or hostile — communicates with its controller through radio waves (RF signals). Just like every human heart has a unique rhythm, every drone has a unique radio fingerprint.
AegisDrone listens to those radio waves with a special antenna, and its AI brain analyses the signal 83 different ways simultaneously — in under 100 milliseconds — and tells you:

🟢 Friendly — known, safe drone
🔴 Threat — hostile, unknown, or suspicious drone
⚪ Background noise — birds, Wi-Fi, weather
❓ Unknown — never seen before, being monitored
⏸️ Hold — AI is not sure yet, gathering more data

And if it detects a threat — it automatically activates countermeasures (jamming the drone's control signal) without waiting for human approval.

How Does It Know What It's Looking At?
The Stethoscope Analogy
A doctor doesn't just hear "thump thump" — they hear rate, rhythm, strength, irregularity. Similarly, our system doesn't just detect "there's a radio signal" — it measures 83 specific characteristics of that signal.
Let's call these the 83 Questions the AI asks about every signal it hears:


How strong is this signal? (like asking: is this a shout or a whisper?)
How wide does it spread across frequencies? (is it a narrow laser beam or a wide flashlight?)
How complex is its pattern? (is it a simple beep or a complex song?)
How does its energy distribute across high and low frequencies? (does it lean high or low?)
How stable is it over time? (does it stay the same or jump around?)


A background noise like rain or birds gives weak, simple, low-complexity answers to these 83 questions.
A consumer drone like a DJI Phantom gives strong, complex, high-frequency answers.
A threat drone behaves differently from any known friendly drone.
The AI has learned — by studying thousands of examples — exactly what "normal" looks like. Anything that deviates from normal gets flagged.

How Does It Tell Friendly From Threat?
The Border Checkpoint Analogy
Think of it like a smart security checkpoint at an international airport.
Step 1 — Does this person even belong here? (Open-Set Check)
If a signal looks nothing like any known drone — it's immediately flagged as unknown. Like a passport from a country that doesn't exist.
Step 2 — Check the database. (Memory Match)
If this signal has been seen before and cleared — it's waved through instantly. Like a frequent flyer with a known identity.
Step 3 — Run through full security. (AI Analysis)
Five different AI models independently examine the signal, vote on what it is, and combine their verdict. Like having five security experts each give their independent assessment before a final call is made.
Step 4 — Watch behaviour over time. (Temporal Tracking)
A new drone that keeps appearing consistently, with the same radio pattern, and no threatening behaviour — gets added to the trusted list automatically. Like an unknown visitor who checks in daily for a week, behaves perfectly, and eventually gets a visitor's pass.
Step 5 — Activate response if threat confirmed. (Action Controller)
If threat is confirmed — jammer activates automatically. No waiting for a phone call up the chain of command.

The "No Retraining" Question — Why This Matters
What Competitors Do (The Old Way)
Traditional drone detection systems are trained on, say, 50 known drone models. When a new drone model enters the market — which happens every 3 months in the commercial drone industry — those systems go blind. You have to:

Collect thousands of new recordings of the new drone
Send them to a lab
Retrain the AI model (takes days to weeks)
Re-deploy the updated system
Repeat for every new drone that appears

This is exactly like having a security guard who can only recognise 50 faces and needs to go back to training school every time someone new arrives.
What AegisDrone Does (Our Approach)
Our system learns at the field, in real time, without going back to the lab.
Here's how it gains confidence in an unknown drone:

Day 1: Unknown drone appears. AI flags it ❓. Starts watching.
Day 2: Same drone appears again. Same radio fingerprint. Low threat behaviour. Trust score rises.
Day 4: Seen 4 times. Consistent. Not threatening. Automatically promoted to "trusted." Stored in memory database permanently.
Day 5 onwards: Recognised instantly. No AI processing needed. Waved through in milliseconds.

The AI model weights never change. But the memory database grows. Think of it like a security guard whose training never changes — but whose notebook of known faces keeps growing every day.
This means:

✅ No lab. No data collection. No retraining delay.
✅ Works in remote locations with no internet connectivity
✅ Adapts to new threats without central intervention
✅ Each deployment becomes smarter over time


