Limitation 2: Promotion Thresholds Are Currently Permissive
Current reality: A drone can get promoted to "trusted" after being seen just once with 20% trust score.
Resolution path: For military deployment, we recommend setting PROMO_MIN_OBS = 10 and PROMO_TRUST_THR = 0.75 — requiring 10 consistent sightings and 75% trust score before promotion. These are configuration changes, not code changes.
Analogy: Like deciding how many times you check someone's ID before giving them a permanent pass. We've given the government a dial — they decide how strict it should be.

Limitation 3: The Jammer is Currently a Mock
Current reality: The software says "jam this drone" but doesn't yet connect to real jamming hardware.
Resolution path: The code has a clearly defined hardware interface. Any standard RF jamming hardware (like BEL's existing systems) can be connected by replacing 10 lines of code. We are hardware-agnostic by design — we can integrate with whatever BEL, DRDO, or L&T produces.

Limitation 4: No Visual Confirmation
Current reality: The system works purely on RF signals. It cannot visually confirm the drone's payload.
Resolution path: Integration with PTZ cameras and optical AI (a separate module) to provide visual confirmation when RF threat is detected. This is Phase 2 of the project roadmap.

