          → Final exam — AI sees these for the first time
          → Results here are what gets reported
Why Three Splits?
Think of it like preparing students for a board exam:

Training set = the textbook and practice problems the student studies
Validation set = mock exams used to tune how the teacher grades
Test set = the actual board exam the student has never seen

If you use the exam paper for practice, the student's score is meaningless. Same here — the test set is locked away until final evaluation.

What Happens to Training Data Before Training?
The raw training data goes through three enrichment steps before any AI model sees it.
Step 1: Mixup Augmentation
Imagine blending two photos — 70% photo of a dog, 30% photo of a cat. You get a slightly dog-like, slightly cat-like image. The AI learns that the space between classes also needs to be understood.
pythonMIXUP_ALPHA = 0.30   # 30% background mixed into drone signal
MIXUP_N_PER_CLASS = 800  # creates 800 such mixed examples per drone class
This adds 1,600 new artificial training examples that help the AI handle the real-world situation where a drone is flying near interference.
Step 2: Hard Negative Mining
After the first round of training, the system finds the examples the AI got most wrong — the ones it was most confused about — and adds jittered (slightly varied) copies of those back into training.
Like a teacher identifying the questions a student keeps getting wrong and drilling those specifically.
pythonHARD_NEG_PERCENTILE = 20   # bottom 20% most-confused examples get recycled
HARD_NEG_JITTER = 0.08     # slight random variation added so it's not just memorising
Step 3: SMOTE Balancing
If one class has fewer examples than others, the AI naturally ignores it. SMOTE creates synthetic in-between examples of the minority class to balance everything out.
Like photocopying rare exam questions so students practice them as much as common ones.

What Are the Five AI Models Trained On?
Each of the five models gets a different view of the same data — like five specialists each reviewing different test results of the same patient:
ModelWhat Features It SeesWhyLGB-RF (Random Forest)Top 45 most informative featuresBroad overview — sees the most relevant signalsLGB-GBT (Gradient Boosting)Top 40 most variable featuresFocuses on what changes most between classesLogistic RegressionAll 83 featuresSimple linear relationships across everything1D CNN (Neural Network)All 83 featuresLearns non-linear patterns humans can't describeGBP (Bayesian model)All 83 featuresGives probability estimates with uncertainty
The feature selection (which 45 out of 83 to give the Random Forest) is done automatically using Mutual Information — a mathematical measure of "how much does knowing this feature tell me about which class this is?"

Training vs Test Performance — What Gets Measured
On the test set (data the AI has never seen), the system measures:
Drone Detection Recall:   target ≥ 85%
  → Of all real threat drones, what % did we catch?

False Alarm Rate:          target ≤ 10%
  → Of all safe signals, what % did we wrongly flag as threat?

Hold Fraction:             target ≤ 20%
  → What % of signals did the AI say "not sure yet"?

Flicker Index:             target < 0.65
  → How often does the AI flip its decision back and forth?

Open-Set Fraction:         target ≥ 4%
  → What % of signals did we correctly flag as unknown/novel?
All five must pass for the system to be declared production ready.

