From Section 13 Quickstart:
→ Evaluates 1600 test samples
You do NOT have 5-fold cross-validation. That is true.
However, the README provides strong justification for why the single split is still valid:
Justification 1 — Scale: 7,998 windows total. A 1,600-sample test set is 20% of the data. That is a substantial held-out set for a 3-class problem.
Justification 2 — Stratification: The split is stratified by class, meaning class proportions are preserved. This is the correct way to split imbalanced multi-class data.
Justification 3 — Multiple evaluation dimensions: Instead of cross-validation, you validated across six different operational metrics, three stress tests, and 38 unit tests. This is arguably more meaningful for a deployed system than CV accuracy alone.
Justification 4 — The stress tests act as a form of distribution shift validation:

Ghost Hunt: same emitter under noise → 0 transitions
Adversarial: pure random noise → 99.5% safe
Recovery: fresh emitter → stable in 0.2s

These test generalisation in ways CV cannot.
How to answer if asked about cross-validation:

"We used a stratified 80/20 train-test split with a held-out validation set for threshold calibration. Cross-validation was not applied because the evaluation protocol goes beyond accuracy — we validate across six operational gates, three adversarial stress tests, and 38 unit tests including distribution shift scenarios. The 1,600-sample test set, held completely separate from training and threshold calibration, provides sufficient statistical power for the 3-class problem. Cross-validation is on our roadmap for the certification protocol detailed in Section 12."

That answer is honest, complete, and will satisfy most evaluators.

